Template: inputloop-gitcha/public_url
Type: string
Default: https://gitcha.example.org
Description: Public URL of this Gitcha server:
 The address people and agents use to reach this server: the TLS proxy in
 front of it, so normally https://.  Secure cookies are on when it starts
 with https://.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/hosting_domains
Type: string
Default:
Description: Domains that hosted sites get subdomains on (may be empty):
 A comma list, for example pages.example.net,apps.example.net.  Use
 registrable domains other than the public URL's, so a site's scripts cannot
 set cookies for Gitcha.  Empty offers no platform subdomains.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/store
Type: select
Choices: cassandra, file
Default: @STORE_DEFAULT@
Description: Where should Gitcha keep its database?
 cassandra: an Apache Cassandra keyspace (the next questions say which).
 .
 file: one file, /var/lib/gitcha/db.json.
 .
 Blobs, git repositories, sites and secret.key stay in /var/lib/gitcha either
 way.  When an empty Cassandra instance finds a db.json there, it imports it.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_setup
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Install Apache Cassandra on this machine?
 No Cassandra is installed here.  If this is accepted, then once this
 installation has finished: Apache's apt repository for Cassandra (debian.cassandra.apache.org,
 Cassandra 5.0) is added with Apache's signing key, Cassandra and the Java it
 needs are installed and started, and Gitcha uses it at 127.0.0.1.
 .
 It needs network access to apache.org and about 4 GB of memory beside Gitcha.
 Follow it with: journalctl -u gitcha-setup-cassandra -f
 .
 Otherwise the next questions ask where your Cassandra nodes are.  One can
 also be set up here later with: sudo gitcha-setup-cassandra

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_contact_points
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1
Description: Cassandra nodes to connect to:
 Host names or addresses, comma separated, each with an optional :port (9042
 when none is given).  A few nodes are enough: the driver finds the rest.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_datacenter
Type: string
Default: datacenter1
Description: Cassandra data center of those nodes:
 Cassandra's own default is datacenter1.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_keyspace
Type: string
Default: gitcha
Description: Cassandra keyspace:
 Created at the first start if it does not exist yet.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_instance
Type: string
Default: main
Description: Name of this Gitcha server inside the keyspace:
 Several Gitcha servers may share one keyspace, each under a name of its own
 (a letter, then letters, digits or _).  Only one running server may use a
 name at a time.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_replication
Type: string
Default: SimpleStrategy:1
Description: Replication for a new keyspace:
 Used only when Gitcha creates the keyspace: SimpleStrategy:1 for a single
 node, or for example NetworkTopologyStrategy:dc1=3 for three copies in data
 center dc1.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_username
Type: string
Default:
Description: Cassandra user name (empty: no authentication):

Template: inputloop-gitcha/cassandra_password
Type: password
Description: Cassandra password:
 Kept in /etc/inputloop/gitcha/debconf.env (readable by root and the gitcha
 group only), not in the debconf database.  Leave it empty to keep the one
 already set.

Template: inputloop-gitcha/enable
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Enable and start the gitcha service now?
 Gitcha listens on 127.0.0.1:4873 only; put a TLS proxy in front of it
 (/usr/share/doc/inputloop-gitcha/Caddyfile.example).  If not now, later:
 systemctl enable --now gitcha
